{
  "trace_id": "hybrid::q10",
  "architecture": "hybrid",
  "question": "Why does a golden dataset need to stay fixed rather than being regenerated each time a system is evaluated?",
  "answer": "A golden dataset must remain fixed because its usefulness comes from providing a **stable yardstick** against which every change to a RAG system can be measured. If the test set drifts each time you evaluate, you can no longer tell whether a score change reflects a real improvement or simply a different (and possibly easier) set of questions\u202f\u2014\u202fthe evaluation becomes meaningless. Keeping the same questions and answers lets you re\u2011run the exact same test after altering chunking, embeddings, prompts, or other components and see whether the change truly helped or just shuffled which questions pass\u202f\u3010building-a-golden-dataset-for-rag-evaluation::0\u3011. This stability also enables reliable tracking of metrics such as retrieval accuracy, citation quality, and refusal behavior over time\u3010ai-evaluation-for-personal-knowledge-bases::1\u3011, and supports versioning so historical scores stay comparable even as the corpus evolves\u3010building-a-golden-dataset-for-rag-evaluation::1\u3011.",
